 Nvidia employees get 15% stock discount, 8 extra days off pa: HR chief on staff retention
 The perks help explain why employee turnover at the AI chipmaker remains far below the industry average even as demand for talent intensifies across the artificial intelligence sector.
 Curated by : 
 Ankita Sengupta 
 October 08, 2026 / 20:52 IST 
 --> 
 HR director Febe Punsalan says Nvidia maintains a relatively flat organisational structure, with employees encouraged to share ideas regardless of seniority. 
 Nvidia's soaring stock price may have created thousands of employee millionaires, but the AI giant says its retention strategy goes beyond compensation.
Speaking to Fortune , Nvidia HR director Febe Punsalan said one of the company's biggest employee incentives is its stock purchase programme, which allows workers to buy Nvidia shares at a 15% discount using their salary. The benefit comes alongside company-wide "free days," additional time off introduced after employee feedback during the Covid-19 pandemic.
The perks help explain why employee turnover at the AI chipmaker remains far below the industry average even as demand for talent intensifies across the AI sector.
 15 percent stock discount creates powerful incentive 
Nvidia, which has grown into one of the world's most valuable companies with a market capitalization of about $5.65 trillion, allows employees to purchase company stock at a 15 percent market discount.
The programme has proven especially lucrative as Nvidia's shares have surged amid the AI boom, turning many employees into multimillionaires. The rewards, however, come with high expectations. Punsalan acknowledged that Nvidia is a demanding workplace. 
“When you come to work for Nvidia, it's a challenging place, and it's challenging work,” she said. “But it's meaningful work.”
Some former and current employees have previously described the company as a "pressure cooker" environment, with long working hours and intense performance demands.
Beyond stock-based compensation, Nvidia offers what it calls "free days." Every quarter, the entire company shuts down for two additional days, allowing employees to disconnect without worrying about work piling up.
“The entire company shuts down,” Punsalan said, adding that employees return without facing inboxes full of messages from colleagues who continued working.
Punsalan said Nvidia's culture is guided by the belief that "the mission is always the boss." The company maintains a relatively flat organisational structure, with employees encouraged to share ideas regardless of seniority.
That approach appears to be resonating with workers. Nvidia's global employee turnover rate stands at just 3.7 percent, compared with the technology industry average of 21.9 percent, Fortune reported.
