India's trade agreements with the UK and EU will play a crucial role in driving Amazon's exports from India and the company is confident of achieving its $80-billion cumulative target by 2030, Srinidhi Kalvapudi, head of Amazon Global Selling India, has told Moneycontrol in an interview.
The e-commerce giant crossed $20 billion in cumulative ecommerce exports from India in 2025 and is now looking to add another $60 billion over the next five years, with new markets, a wider seller base and manufacturing clusters expected to drive the next phase of growth.
“We are very confident about the $80 billion target by 2030,” Kalvapudi told Moneycontrol . “The next $60 billion can come from the EU, it can come from the UK, it can come from newer markets. It can come from existing sellers, it can come from new sellers.”
The India-UK trade agreement has moved into implementation, while the EU deal is expected to become operational by next year, potentially opening up further opportunities for Indian exporters.
 US tariff changes not in Amazon’s control 
The comments come at a time when Indian exporters have been navigating changes in US tariff policy, creating uncertainty for businesses that depend on the American market.
Kalvapudi, however, said Amazon remained confident of meeting its export target, pointing to the underlying strengths of its business and the supply chains developed by Indian sellers over the years.
“Again, our sort of numbers is speaking for themselves. We did $20 billion in 2025, when we were seeing a lot of the tariff-related changes,” he said. “Tariffs are, of course, uncontrollable for anybody. I don't think our organisation has any influence.”
Amazon’s consumer exports business was supported by an established supply chain and long-term structural strengths rather than temporary market conditions.
“One thing I would like to point out is consumer exports. We have been exporting over time, we have an established supply chain and the strength is structural, not cyclical. So, I remain fully confident about the $80 billion target by 2030,” he said.
Amazon sees the EU and the UK as important markets for the next phase of exports from India.
Kalvapudi said the company was assessing where Indian sellers could find demand and how they could expand their presence. The opportunity spans established export categories as well as products that could gain wider recognition among overseas consumers.
Amazon sees home textiles, cotton bedding, home décor, apparel, carpets and rugs, furniture, health and personal care and ayurveda among the categories that have potential across global markets.
Through Amazon Global Selling, the company helps Indian sellers identify products and demand in overseas markets. It also provides support for advertising, deals, logistics and compliance. Some of these services are facilitated through third-party service providers.
The company believes the opportunity is not restricted to large businesses or sellers based in India's biggest cities. A growing part of the export opportunity is coming from tier-2 and 3 cities, where businesses are drawing on manufacturing capabilities to reach international customers.
Kalvapudi said India's manufacturing clusters could play an important role in scaling exports, given their existing expertise in producing goods for domestic and international markets.
Tiruppur in Tamil Nadu, for instance, is a major textile manufacturing hub. Other examples include furniture manufacturing in Rajasthan, towel and textile production around Ichalkaranji in Maharashtra and textile clusters in Haryana.
These clusters give sellers access to specialised production capabilities and established supply chains. For Amazon, expanding participation among such sellers will be important to meet its export ambitions.
 India’s export opportunity goes beyond low-cost products 
Kalvapudi said one of the more notable developments in ecommerce exports was the willingness of overseas consumers to buy higher-value Indian products, challenging the perception that India's export competitiveness rests primarily on low prices.
He said he was not surprised that consumers around the world wanted Indian products. What was more surprising was the perception that this demand was largely driven by the availability of cheaper goods.
